# frozen_string_literal: true

# SB-2140 Stage 1 — commit-ordered sequence ("commit_seq").
#
# ## Why
#
# `sequence` (BIGSERIAL via nextval) is assigned at INSERT, inside the producer
# transaction, but rows only become visible at COMMIT. Under concurrency,
# assignment order != commit order, so a lower-`sequence` row that commits AFTER
# a higher one on the same partition lands BELOW the consumer's high-water offset
# and is silently never delivered.
#
# ## What this migration installs
#
# * outbox_relay_partition_seq — a transactional per-(topic, partition) sequencer.
# * outbox_relay_outbox_events.commit_seq — the new ordering token (nullable here;
#   the optional harden_commit_seq migration adds a fail-loud assert trigger).
# * A DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED, per-ROW, AFTER INSERT constraint trigger that
#   assigns commit_seq at the COMMIT edge.
#
# This migration is SCHEMA-ONLY and fast — it does NOT backfill existing rows.
# After it runs, new inserts get commit_seq via the trigger; backfill the
# historical rows out of band with `rake outbox_relay:backfill_commit_seq`
# (throttleable, resumable). The backfill must finish before the Stage 2 consumer
# flip.
#
# ## Trigger shape (why per-row + advisory lock)
#
# PostgreSQL only allows DEFERRABLE on a CONSTRAINT TRIGGER, and a CONSTRAINT
# TRIGGER must be FOR EACH ROW and may NOT use REFERENCING ... TABLE (transition
# tables) — so the statement-level form is impossible. We therefore use a per-row
# deferred trigger.
#
# A bare per-row trigger that only locks its own partition's sequencer row would
# DEADLOCK: deferred triggers fire at commit in row-insertion order, each holds a
# partition row lock until commit, and two concurrent multi-partition transactions
# on the same topic (the drainer workload) acquire those locks in different orders.
# To stay deadlock-free we take a per-TOPIC advisory xact lock at the top of the
# trigger (two-int form — a separate lock space from the consumer's bigint advisory
# locks). All of a transaction's rows for a topic share that one re-entrant lock,
# so commit-edge assignment serializes per topic (deadlock-free), while different
# topics proceed in parallel. First-to-commit takes the lock first, so per
# (topic, partition): commit_seq order == commit order == visibility order.
#
# ## Caveats
#
# * Triggers do NOT fire under `session_replication_role = 'replica'` (logical
#   replication / DMS / blue-green apply). For an app-written outbox this is fine.
# * A bug in the trigger fails publishes at COMMIT (fail-closed; at-least-once
#   preserved). Verify on staging before production.
#
# Run with: rails generate outbox_relay:add_commit_seq
class AddCommitSeqToOutboxRelayOutboxEvents < ActiveRecord::Migration<%= migration_version %>
  disable_ddl_transaction!

  def up
    create_partition_seq_table
    unless column_exists?(:outbox_relay_outbox_events, :commit_seq)
      add_column :outbox_relay_outbox_events, :commit_seq, :bigint
    end

    # Install the trigger. CREATE TRIGGER takes its own brief lock atomically, so
    # an INSERT is either fully before it (commit_seq NULL → caught by the backfill
    # below) or fully after (trigger fires) — no half state, no explicit table lock
    # needed. We do NOT pre-seed existing partitions: the trigger lazily seeds each
    # partition's sequencer row from the global sequence high-water on its first
    # insert (same result), which avoids a full-table scan under a write-blocking
    # lock on a large table.
    #
    # CAUTION on very busy tables / long-running transactions: CREATE TRIGGER must
    # briefly acquire SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE on the table. If a long transaction (e.g.
    # a bulk import) is in flight it will wait behind it, and new writes queue
    # behind the waiting DDL. Run during a quiet window, or set a short lock_timeout
    # and retry, so a stuck acquisition doesn't stall publishers.
    safety_assured do
      execute create_function_sql
      execute create_trigger_sql
    end

    add_commit_seq_index

    # NOTE: historical rows are NOT backfilled here. From now on new inserts get a
    # commit_seq via the trigger; existing rows stay NULL until you run the
    # throttleable, resumable backfill task OUT of band:
    #
    #   bundle exec rake outbox_relay:backfill_commit_seq
    #
    # Keeping a 1M+ row rewrite out of the migration avoids a long, unmonitorable,
    # un-resumable deploy step. The backfill MUST complete (zero committed NULL
    # commit_seq) before the Stage 2 consumer flip — Stage 1 consumers still read
    # `sequence`, so NULLs are harmless until then.
  end

  def down
